course: books
lesson: 32
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter II, part 4: The Open Road (the wreck of the cart, and Toad's new craze)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The frightened old horse rears and backs, and drags the cart into the deep ditch: crash! The lovely canary-coloured cart lies on its side, smashed beyond repair, sardine tins everywhere and the bird in its cage sobbing to be let out.
explain: The Rat dances in the road shaking his fists at the vanished car: «You villains! ... you roadhogs! I'll have the law of you!» But Toad just sits in the middle of the road, staring after the car with a happy, dreamy smile, murmuring «Poop-poop!»
explain: He won't help. «The poetry of motion! The real way to travel!» Carts are «horrid little carts» to him now. The Rat knows the signs: Toad has a new craze, and for days he'll be useless, like an animal walking in a happy dream.
explain: They walk five or six miles to the nearest town, Toad trailing after them, thanking the Rat for bringing him on the trip where he saw that «swan, that sunbeam, that thunderbolt». They leave him with a porter at the station, catch a slow train home, hand Toad to his housekeeper and row back to their own cosy parlour.
explain: The next evening the Rat brings the news everyone along the river is talking about: Toad took an early train to Town and has ordered a large and very expensive motor-car.
fact: The horse, «dreaming ... of his quiet paddock», was so frightened that «Rearing, plunging, backing steadily», he drove the cart backwards into «the deep ditch at the side of the road».
fact: «the canary-coloured cart, their pride and their joy, lay on its side in the ditch, an irredeemable wreck» (irredeemable = impossible to put right).
fact: The Rat shouted: «You villains! ... You scoundrels, you highwaymen, you—you—roadhogs!—I'll have the law of you! I'll report you! I'll take you through all the Courts!» (a roadhog = a selfish, dangerous driver; highwaymen = old-time robbers on the roads).
fact: For a moment the Rat felt like a ship's captain whose boat had been driven aground by reckless sailors — remembering what he used to shout at steam-launches whose waves flooded «his parlour-carpet at home».
fact: «Toad sat straight down in the middle of the dusty road ... his face wore a placid satisfied expression, and at intervals he faintly murmured "Poop-poop!"» (placid = calm).
fact: The cart: «Panels and windows smashed, axles hopelessly bent, one wheel off, sardine-tins scattered over the wide world, and the bird in the bird-cage sobbing pitifully».
fact: Toad: «Glorious, stirring sight! ... The poetry of motion! The real way to travel! The only way to travel! Here to-day—in next week to-morrow! ... O bliss! O poop-poop! O my! O my!»
fact: Toad: «What carts I shall fling carelessly into the ditch in the wake of my magnificent onset! Horrid little carts—common carts—canary-coloured carts!»
fact: The Rat: «He is now possessed. He has got a new craze, and it always takes him that way, in its first stage. He'll continue like that for days now, like an animal walking in a happy dream, quite useless for all practical purposes.»
fact: The Mole: «We can't leave him here ... Supposing another Thing were to come along?» The Rat: «O, bother Toad ... I've done with him!» — but Toad came pattering after them.
fact: The Rat's plan: police station to «lodge a complaint», a blacksmith or wheelwright (a wheel-maker) to mend the cart. Toad: «Me complain of that beautiful, that heavenly vision ... that swan, that sunbeam, that thunderbolt! ... I owe it all to you, my best of friends!»
fact: At the station they left Toad «in the second-class waiting-room, giving a porter twopence to keep a strict eye on him», and left the horse at an inn stable.
fact: They «instructed his housekeeper to feed him, undress him, and put him to bed», then sculled home to «their own cosy riverside parlour, to the Rat's great joy».
fact: The chapter ends: «Toad went up to Town by an early train this morning. And he has ordered a large and very expensive motor-car.»
fact: Old words: «transported with passion» = carried away with anger; «trance» = a dream-like state while awake; «vouchsafed» = granted, given as a gift; «a-pleasuring» = on a pleasure trip; «provoking» = annoying; «Town» = London.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «gurt» (West Country) = great, big; «proper job» (West Country) = well done; «aye» (Scotland, North) = yes; «skibidi» = a silly word kids shout, meaning nothing; «I'm dead» = that's hilarious; «washroom» (Canada) = toilet.
exercise: What makes the horse rear and back? | The motor-car roaring past | A rabbit jumping out of the hedge | The Rat shouting «Whoa!» too loudly | A bee stinging him on the nose
exercise: Where does the cart end up? | On its side in the deep ditch | In the river, floating away like a boat | On the car | In a field, the right way up
exercise: «an irredeemable wreck». What does «irredeemable» mean? | Impossible to put right | Easy to fix with a hammer and some nails | Brand new | Bright yellow
exercise: What does the Rat call the people in the car? | Villains, scoundrels and roadhogs | Kind gentlemen who were in a terrible hurry to get to Town | Toad's friends | Nothing
exercise: «I'll have the law of you!» What does the Rat mean? | He'll take them to court | He'll write a law book about them | He'll forgive them in the end, as friends do | He'll race them along the road to Town
exercise: What does Toad do after the crash? | Sits in the road murmuring «Poop-poop!» | Jumps down into the ditch to rescue the poor sobbing bird | Shouts | Cries
exercise: Who is sobbing in the wreck? | The bird in the bird-cage | The old grey horse | Toad | The Mole
exercise: Which is NOT in the list of damage? | The horse's leg broken | Panels and windows smashed | Axles bent | One wheel off
exercise: What does Toad call the motor-car's way of moving? | «The poetry of motion!» | «A danger to us all» | «Silly boyish amusement» | «Messing about»
exercise: What does Toad now call carts? | «Horrid little carts—common carts» | «The real life» | «The finest in the world» | «Lovely»
exercise: How does the Rat explain Toad's state? | He's «possessed» by a new craze | He banged his head and needs a doctor at once | He's tired | He's ill
exercise: How long does the Rat say Toad will stay like this? | For days | For the rest of his life | For one hour | Until tea-time
exercise: How far is it to the nearest town? | Five or six miles | Only about half a mile | Fifty miles or more | Two hundred miles away
exercise: What does the Rat say when the Mole worries about leaving Toad? | «O, bother Toad ... I've done with him!» | «Of course, let's carry him all the way» | «Call the police» | «Toad can fly»
exercise: What three things does Toad call the car? | A swan, a sunbeam, a thunderbolt | A bee, a dragon and a lion | A boat, a cart, a horse | A star
exercise: Who does Toad thank for the trip? | The Rat | The Mole | The horse | The man driving the motor-car
exercise: Where do they leave Toad in the town? | The second-class waiting-room at the station | The police station, to lodge a complaint about the car | An inn | The blacksmith's
exercise: How much do they give the porter to watch Toad? | Twopence | Sixpence a day | A whole shilling | Nothing at all
exercise: Where is the horse left? | At an inn stable | At the station with Toad | Back at Toad Hall in his paddock | Tied up by the ditch
exercise: Who puts Toad to bed? | His housekeeper | The Rat, after a hot bath and a story | The Mole, very gently | The porter at the station
exercise: Where do the Rat and the Mole have supper that night? | Their own riverside parlour | A grand hotel in Town, near the motor-car shop | Toad Hall | The common
exercise: What is the news the next evening? | Toad has ordered a large, expensive motor-car | Toad has mended the cart and set off again on the open road | Toad is ill | Toad has gone boating
test: Ben, gurt (big) fan of Toad: «Toad helped push the cart out of the ditch, proper job.» (proper job = well done) Put it right. | He wouldn't budge from the road | Right: he pushed harder than both of the others together | He wasn't there | He fixed it alone
test: Why is Toad smiling while his cart lies smashed? | He's in love with the motor-car | He's glad the cart is broken because it was ugly | He's in shock and doesn't see it | He thinks it's funny
test: «Here to-day—in next week to-morrow!» What is Toad trying to say? | Cars are so fast you'd be days ahead | He's muddled up the days of the week | Tomorrow is next week | He wants to stay
test: The Rat says Toad is «possessed». Has the Rat seen this before? | Yes — «it always takes him that way» | No, this is the first time Toad has ever had a craze | He's never met Toad | Only in a book
test: Why does the Mole not want to leave Toad in the road? | Another car might come — it's not safe | He wants to sit with him and watch for motor-cars too | He likes Toad more than the Rat | He's tired
test: Chloe, aye (yes) sure: «Toad complained to the police about the car.» Is she right? | No — he says «Me complain» of that «heavenly vision»! | Yes: he went straight to the police station and lodged a long complaint against the driver | Yes, the next day | No: the Rat did
test: Toad says to the Rat «I owe it all to you, my best of friends!» Why is this so annoying for the Rat? | The trip wrecked everything | Toad is being very rude to him and calling him names in front of the Mole | The Rat wanted the car | Toad owes him money
test: «And if ever you catch me going a-pleasuring with this provoking animal again!» What is the Rat promising himself? | Never to go on a trip with Toad again | To go on a trip every summer with Toad | To buy a car | To stay at Toad Hall
test: Looking back: what did the Rat say about Toad in part 1 — «Always good-tempered, always glad to see you». Has Toad been good company today? | No — selfish, dreamy and no help at all | Yes, he has helped with every single job and thanked them | He was angry | He was shy
test: Looking back: at the start of the trip, Toad said carts were «the real life». What does he call carts now? | «Horrid little carts—common carts» | «The poetry of motion» | «The only way to travel» | «Lovely little homes on wheels»
test: Looking back: on the first night the Rat thought about his river «all the time». How does the chapter end for him? | Home in his parlour, to his great joy | Lost on the road, far from the river, with a broken cart and no horse | In a motor-car | At Toad Hall
test: Looking back: in chapter I the Otter laughed «no stability» about Toad. What proves it in this chapter? | Boats, then a cart, now a car — all in one summer | He keeps the cart and mends it to use again next spring | He stays at home | He's steady
test: Looking back: in chapter I, who flooded the Rat's parlour-carpet with their waves? | Steam-launches near the bank | The Otter, splashing about after May-flies in the backwater | Toad's wager-boat | Ducks
test: ★★★ Toad's crazes in order, from chapter I to the end of chapter II. Which list is right? | Sailing, punting, house-boat, wager-boat, caravan, motor-car | Caravan, sailing, motor-car, punting | Motor-car, caravan, boats | Punting, sailing, caravan, house-boat, motor-car
test: ★★★ «bottles were certainly flung, and from steamers» (chapter I) and «roadhogs» (now). What do the Rat's two complaints share? | Careless people with fast machines | Both are about the rabbits who live in the hedgerows by the road and the river | The Wild Wood | Ducks
test: ★★★ «I've done with carts for ever.» Toad said almost the same about boats earlier. What can we guess about the motor-car? | He'll probably tire of it too — or it will get him into trouble | It will be his hobby for ever, safely | He'll never drive it | He'll give it to the Rat
exam: Identify the immediate cause of the overturning of the canary-coloured cart. | The terror of the horse occasioned by the passing motor-car | The negligence of the Mole | A broken wheel | A collision with a second cart
exam: Ascertain the demeanour of Toad in the moments following the disaster. | A happy trance, murmuring «Poop-poop!» | He was overcome with grief at the destruction of his cherished cart | He pursued the car | He scolded the horse
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the Rat's diagnosis of Toad's condition. | That he was possessed by a new craze and would be useless for some days | That he was injured | That he had lost his memory | That he was feigning
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the conclusion of the second chapter? | Toad travelled to Town and ordered a large and very expensive motor-car | Toad lodged a complaint against the motorist | The cart was restored | The Rat purchased a motor-car
